--
N5008 [temp.inst]/2: a class template specialization is implicitly
instantiated only when a completely-defined object type is required;
[temp.alias]/2: `rebind<node<void>>' is `node<void> *', which does not
require node<void> to be complete; [class.member.lookup]: node<void>'s
non-dependent base node_base<int> supplies `link_pointer'. The front end
instantiated node<void> while substituting it into the alias template,
which happens inside node_traits<int>, which is instantiated while
node_base<int> is being defined; node<void> then saw an incomplete base
and reported "symbol 'link_pointer' is unknown" (and later "symbol
'as_link' is unknown"). Writing `node<void> *' directly works. This is
libc++'s list: __list_node_pointer_traits rebinds through
pointer_traits<void *>::rebind to __list_node / __list_node_base, and
__list_node::__as_link (list:271ff) and list::push_back were dropped, so
push_back gave a wrong verdict (test_libcxx.desc, the std::list program).
Header-free (cvise, /tmp/r163/red/kb_S and kb_S6). Naming arguments of an
alias that denotes a pointer to a parameter, or forwards to a qualified
member template-id, no longer instantiates them (skipping it for every
alias broke cpp17_tuple_size_selected_despite_cv_partial_specializations
and std::function). Fails on bin53.
